DAYS = [
 dict(n=19, f='psy', title='The crowd is not a character', ep=(CCY, 37, 'h6HLDV0T5Q8', '10:48', 'Social Thinking'), img='zimbardo',
      imgcap='Philip Zimbardo photographed in 2017. The Stanford Prison Experiment became a story about roles, but its archive became a warning about how stories, leaders and situations shape behaviour.',
      imgalt='Philip Zimbardo speaking at a 2017 forum',
      hook='In August 1971, twenty-four young men entered a mock prison in Stanford psychology department basement. Some were randomly assigned guards, others prisoners. The famous story says that ordinary people became cruel because the situation made them so, and that the study had to stop after six days. The stranger truth is that the archive shows experimenters giving guards instructions and the participants were not simply swallowed by their roles.',
      q='When someone behaves badly in a group, how much belongs to the person, the situation and the group story?',
      terms=[('Social cognition','how people perceive, explain and remember other people and groups'),('Attribution','an explanation of why an event or behaviour happened'),('Fundamental attribution error','overweighting personality and underweighting situation when judging others'),('Social identity','the part of self-concept drawn from membership in groups'),('Identity leadership','leading by defining a shared we and what that we should do')],
      pre=[('True or false: the Stanford Prison Experiment cleanly proved that roles automatically turn decent people cruel.','False. It was influential, but the archive and later analyses found demand, instruction and selection problems. A role can matter without automatically dictating action.'),('If a colleague misses a deadline, is blaming their personality always a fundamental attribution error?','No. Situations matter, but traits can matter too. The error is a bias toward dispositional explanations, not a rule that situations explain everything.')],
      watch=['How the episode separates dispositional explanations from situational ones','The Stanford Prison Experiment story, and why the situation is not a magic switch','The fundamental attribution error and the role of culture in explaining behaviour'],
      fig=D19, figcap='A setting supplies power and rules, but action is mediated by identity and leadership. That is the correction to the simple role story. The attribution trap is the jump from one act to a permanent character verdict.',
      insider=['<b>The Stanford story is not a clean experiment.</b> Le Texier\'s archive study and interviews with 15 participants found biased and incomplete data collection, a prior student prison exercise, precise guard instructions, guards not told they were subjects, and little complete immersion. The study is best labelled partly supported as a demonstration of a powerful narrative and setting, not a decisive test of automatic role transformation. Source: https://doi.org/10.1037/amp0000401',
                '<b>The BBC Prison Study changed the causal spine.</b> Haslam and Reicher randomly assigned real participants to prisoner and guard categories in a television study. Prisoners initially formed a group and challenged the guards; later, a committed faction built a harsher hierarchy. Their account says roles did not automatically produce cruelty. Shared identity, leadership and belief that action served a noble cause mattered. Source: https://doi.org/10.1348/014466605x48998',
                '<b>Zimbardo did not accept the obituary.</b> Zimbardo and Haney answered that dehumanising environments with unequal power can still create destructive dynamics, while rejecting what they called misleading attacks. Haslam, Reicher and Van Bavel replied that archive material shows experimenter identity leadership, and the debate remains about what the data can establish. Source: https://doi.org/10.1037/amp0000593 and https://doi.org/10.1037/amp0000627',
                '<b>The attribution error is culturally shaped, not a Western-only defect.</b> Morris and Peng found American students and English-language newspapers used more dispositional explanations for social events, while Chinese participants and Chinese-language newspapers gave more situational accounts. Physical events did not show the same cultural difference. Later work cautions that culture, language, task and sample all matter. Source: https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.67.6.949'],
      names=[('Thibault Le Texier','Researcher whose 2019 archive study challenged the standard Stanford Prison Experiment textbook story.'),('S. Alexander Haslam and Stephen Reicher','Social psychologists behind the BBC Prison Study and the identity-based account of tyranny.')],
      room='"Before we blame the person, what did the system reward, permit and make visible?"',
      explain=('In 60 words: what is the fair modern lesson of the Stanford Prison Experiment?','The fair lesson is not that roles automatically transform people. The experiment was methodologically compromised, and archive evidence suggests experimenters encouraged tough behaviour. Situations, identities and leaders can shape action, but people interpret situations and may resist. Treat the study as a warning about power and storytelling, not a clean proof of human nature.'),
      recall=[('What did Le Texier\'s archive work add?','It documented demand characteristics, incomplete data, prior prison work, guard instructions, and weak immersion, using archives and interviews with 15 participants.'),('What did the BBC Prison Study suggest?','Group identity and leadership shaped action. Participants first resisted hierarchy, and later a committed group built a harsher system.'),('What did Morris and Peng find across cultures?','American participants and English-language newspapers leaned more toward dispositional explanations, while Chinese participants and Chinese-language newspapers gave more situational explanations for social events.')],
      use=('A clinic client says a receptionist is lazy because she missed three calls during a software outage. How do you respond?','Separate the person from the system. Check staffing, call routing, outage logs, workload and training before judging character. If the pattern survives those checks, discuss performance. This is not excusing missed calls; it is preventing a costly attribution error and improving the process that a client actually pays you to fix.'),
      further=[('Haslam and Reicher, The BBC Prison Study','https://doi.org/10.1348/014466605x48998','Paper · roles, identity and leadership'),('Le Texier, Debunking the Stanford Prison Experiment','https://doi.org/10.1037/amp0000401','Paper · archive-based correction'),('Morris and Peng, Culture and cause','https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.67.6.949','Paper · cross-cultural attribution')]),

 dict(n=20, f='psy', title='The group can lend you a mind', ep=(CCY, 38, 'UGxGDdQnC1Y', '10:08', 'Social Influence'), img='asch',
      imgcap='A diagram of Solomon Asch\'s line-judgement experiment, drawn by Sammie A in 2014. Most people were independent on most trials, which the familiar conformity story often leaves out.',
      imgalt='Diagram of Solomon Asch line judgement conformity experiment',
      hook='Solomon Asch showed people three lines and asked which matched a target. The answer was obvious, until several people in the room unanimously gave the wrong answer. The headline is that one person follows the crowd. The more interesting result is that many did not: most answers on critical trials were independent, and a later replication still found influence without turning everyone into a puppet.',
      q='When does a group help you think, and when does it make a bad idea feel like a duty?',
      terms=[('Conformity','changing a public response to match a group'),('Obedience','acting in response to an authority request or command'),('Informational influence','using others as evidence about what is true'),('Normative influence','matching others to belong, avoid conflict or gain approval'),('Groupthink','a proposed failure mode where cohesion suppresses critical evaluation'),
             ],
      pre=[('True or false: Asch showed that almost everyone conforms when a group is unanimous.','False. The classic data contain substantial independence. In a 2023 replication, the standard line-task error rate was 33 percent, and monetary incentives reduced it to 25 percent, but influence remained.'),('Does Milgram show that people obey blindly?','Not necessarily. Haslam and Reicher argue that many participants were engaged followers who identified with the scientific project and saw continuing as worthwhile. Distress and resistance were part of the record.')],
      watch=['The difference between conformity, compliance and obedience','Asch\'s many non-conformers, and the 2023 replication with N=210','How Milgram, groupthink, polarisation and minority influence fit different mechanisms'],
      fig=D20, figcap='Social influence is not one force. A majority can shift a public answer, a leader can recruit engaged followership, discussion can polarise a starting view, and a consistent minority can open an alternative frame.',
      insider=['<b>Asch is often taught upside down.</b> In the original line experiments, roughly one third of critical responses followed the wrong majority, meaning roughly two thirds were independent. A 2015 textbook analysis found that coverage increasingly emphasised conformity while downplaying independent correct responses. A 2023 replication with 210 participants found a 33 percent error rate in the standard task and 25 percent with incentives. Sources: https://doi.org/10.1177/0098628315569939 and https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0294325',
                '<b>Milgram looks less like a switch labelled obey.</b> Haslam, Reicher and Smith reread the shock studies as engaged followership: willingness changed with cues that made the experimenter and science, or the learner and the wider public, more salient. Across variants, a majority did not go to 450 volts. Gina Perry\'s archival work found that published accounts smoothed the story, and later reanalysis found that in 18 of 23 variations, those who fully believed the learner was in pain gave lower mean shocks. Sources: https://doi.org/10.1177/1745691612448482, https://muse.jhu.edu/pub/17/article/536095 and https://doi.org/10.1177/0190272519861952',
                '<b>Groupthink is a hypothesis, not a magic diagnosis.</b> Janis proposed that high cohesion, insulation and pressure for unanimity can suppress dissent. Reviews warn that empirical tests are fewer and messier than textbook checklists suggest. For you, the practical move is independent pre-mortems, a dissenting role and a record of objections before consensus. Source: https://doi.org/10.1002/bdm.3960030402',
                '<b>Discussion can polarise, and a minority can innovate.</b> Group discussion often shifts members toward a more extreme version of their initial average, through persuasive arguments and social comparison. Moscovici\'s minority research suggests that a consistent minority can create latent change and a new way to see the question. A dissenting designer is not noise if the dissent is informed and steady. Sources: https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.83.4.602 and https://doi.org/10.1002/ejsp.2420060202'],
      names=[('Solomon Asch','1907-1996. His 1950s line studies made public conformity visible, but also documented independence.'),('Stanley Milgram','1933-1984. His shock studies are now reread through archival evidence, ethics and identification-based followership.')],
      room='"Is this agreement evidence, belonging, or just the absence of a safe dissent?"',
      explain=('In 60 words: how can social influence be powerful without being automatic?','People use groups as evidence and as social belonging. A unanimous crowd can make an obvious answer feel socially costly, while an admired leader can make harmful work feel morally valuable. Yet Asch found many independent answers, Milgram recorded resistance, and minorities can introduce new frames. Influence changes probabilities; it does not erase agency.'),
      recall=[('What did Asch actually find?','A substantial minority conformed, but most critical responses were independent. A 2023 replication found a 33 percent error rate, reduced to 25 percent with incentives.'),('What is engaged followership?','People actively identify with a leader or cause and carry out actions they believe serve a worthwhile collective goal, rather than obeying mindlessly.'),('What are groupthink and polarisation?','Groupthink is suppressed critical evaluation under pressure for unanimity. Polarisation is a shift toward a more extreme version of a group\'s initial average after discussion.')],
      use=('Your startup team is choosing a launch date. The founder loves a festival-week launch, everyone nods, and the dissenting developer stays quiet. What do you change?','Separate idea generation from decision, ask each person for a private forecast before the meeting, appoint a red-team owner, and record the strongest objection. Then compare the launch against base rates, staffing and client risk. The goal is not permanent dissent; it is making disagreement safe before the group drifts into groupthink.'),
      further=[('Franzén and Mäder, The power of social influence','https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0294325','Paper · 2023 Asch replication'),('Gina Perry, Behind the Shock Machine','https://doi.org/10.1515/9781595589255','Book · Milgram archives and people'),('Haslam and Reicher, Contesting the nature of conformity','https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.1001426','Paper · engaged followership')]),

 dict(n=21, f='phi', title='Belief is a social act', ep=(CCP, 14, 'AYkhlXronNk', '9:47', 'Anti-Vaxxers, Conspiracy Theories, & Epistemic Responsibility'), img='clifford',
      imgcap='William Kingdon Clifford, whose Ethics of Belief argued that belief has public consequences. The modern question is not whether to trust anyone, but how to calibrate trust and responsibility.',
      imgalt='Public domain portrait of William Kingdon Clifford',
      hook='A conspiracy theory can feel like an explanation, a warning and a badge of membership at once. That is why calling believers stupid misses the mechanism. Research finds epistemic motives such as certainty, existential motives such as control, and social motives such as protecting a group identity. Philosophy then asks a harder question: when evidence is thin, what do we owe other people before we share or act? That is the bridge to No. 03 Cons and cults: persuasion is not only about false claims, but about the social machinery that makes a claim feel like loyalty.',
      q='How should a responsible person believe, share and act when evidence is incomplete?',
      terms=[('Conspiracy theory','an explanation of an important event as a secret plot by powerful actors'),('Epistemic responsibility','a duty to manage belief and testimony with care'),('Epistemic vigilance','checking the reliability of both messages and communicators'),('Motivated reasoning','processing evidence in ways shaped by goals, identity or emotion'),('Unfalsifiability','a claim arranged so that no possible evidence could count against it')],
      pre=[('True or false: a conspiracy theory is simply any claim that two people secretly planned something.','False. Ordinary conspiracies happen. In this research tradition, a conspiracy theory explains significant events through a hidden, powerful and usually malevolent group, often with speculative and self-sealing features.'),('Does epistemic responsibility require certainty before any action?','No. It requires proportion: inspect evidence, disclose uncertainty, seek correction and choose reversible, low-harm actions where confidence is limited.')],
      watch=['The difference between a real conspiracy, a conspiracy theory and a self-sealing story','Clifford and James on the ethics of belief','How epistemic vigilance checks the speaker, the message and the consequences'],
      fig=D21, figcap='A responsible belief pipeline does not demand impossible certainty. It asks where the claim came from, what would test it, how confident to be, and whether the action is proportionate and revisable.',
      insider=['<b>Conspiracy belief is not one kind of gullibility.</b> Douglas, Sutton and Cichocka organise the research around epistemic motives for understanding, existential motives for control and security, and social motives for a positive group image. They caution that evidence for consequences is thinner than evidence for motives, and that a theory can be appealing without satisfying the need that drew someone to it. Source: https://doi.org/10.1177/0963721417718261',
                '<b>Uscinski adds predisposition and cues.</b> In survey and experimental work, conspiratorial predisposition helped explain who accepted a conspiratorial cue, and the predisposition was not reducible to party identity. That means correction is not just dropping facts into an empty vessel. Ask what identity, fear or information gap the story is serving. Source: https://doi.org/10.1177/1065912915621621',
                '<b>Clifford and James disagree about when belief may lead.</b> Clifford\'s 1877 evidentialism says it is wrong to believe on insufficient evidence because beliefs shape conduct and testimony. James\'s 1896 or 1897 pragmatist reply allows a genuine, forced and momentous option to be settled by our passionate nature when intellectual evidence cannot decide it. James is not a licence for every wish: a business claim about a clinic conversion rate is testable, so evidence must lead. Sources: https://doi.org/10.2307/2175898 and https://fisherpub.sjf.edu/bookshelf/90',
                '<b>Epistemic vigilance is social equipment.</b> Sperber and colleagues argue that communication is useful but exposes us to accidental and intentional misinformation, so humans need checks on both content and source. In a client meeting, that means asking who observed the result, what incentive they have, whether the claim could be wrong, and what harm follows if you repeat it. Source: https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1468-0017.2010.01394.x'],
      names=[('Karen Douglas, Robbie Sutton and Aleksandra Cichocka','Researchers who organised conspiracy belief around epistemic, existential and social motives.'),('Dan Sperber','Cognitive scientist whose account of epistemic vigilance explains why trust and suspicion must work together.')],
      room='"What would change my mind, and what harm follows if I am wrong in public?"',
      explain=('In 60 words: what is epistemic responsibility when evidence is incomplete?','It is not waiting for certainty or believing whatever feels useful. It is matching confidence to evidence, checking source and message, looking for disconfirmation, naming uncertainty, and choosing proportionate action. Because beliefs travel through groups, repeating a claim can impose costs on others. A responsible person stays corrigible, especially when identity makes a story satisfying.'),
      recall=[('What three motives organise much conspiracy belief research?','Epistemic motives for understanding, existential motives for control and security, and social motives for a positive image of self or group.'),('What is Clifford\'s central warning?','Belief on insufficient evidence is ethically dangerous because beliefs influence conduct and testimony, so the duty is to inquire responsibly.'),('What does epistemic vigilance check?','It checks the content of a message and the reliability, incentives and trustworthiness of its source.')],
      use=('A new AI vendor tells you a rival agency secretly manipulates search rankings, and offers your studio a rushed six-month contract to fight it. How do you proceed?','Separate the allegation from the contract. Ask for independently verifiable data, alternative explanations, a test that could disconfirm the claim, and the vendor\'s incentives. Do not repeat the accusation as fact. If a small audit is justified, make it bounded and reversible. Responsible scepticism protects your reputation without pretending hidden coordination is impossible.'),
      further=[('Crash Course Philosophy #14, Anti-Vaxxers, Conspiracy Theories, and Epistemic Responsibility','https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AYkhlXronNk','Video · 9:47 · verified Crash Course episode'),('William James, The Will to Believe','https://doi.org/10.2307/2175898','Reference · 1897 essay'),('Douglas, Sutton and Cichocka, The Psychology of Conspiracy Theories','https://doi.org/10.1177/0963721417718261','Paper · motives and consequences')])
]

