 boss=('A brilliant developer joins a high-commitment, cult-like startup. The founder says outsiders are jealous, critics are agents of the old industry, twelve-hour work proves moral seriousness, and every setback proves the team is close to breakthrough. Analyse why a smart person might join and stay, using the whole week. Then propose a responsible intervention that protects the person without humiliating them.',
       '<b>Model answer:</b> Day 15: the startup offers a new identity, a heroic story that makes work feel like a self. Day 16: a founder may over-read personality tests and label loyalty as character, while the developer may use intelligence as evidence that their judgement cannot be captured. Day 17: causes and pressures constrain choice, but responsibility remains in the next decision and in designing exits. Day 18: praise, equity promises and intermittent wins condition overwork. Day 19: do not call the developer irrational; inspect the founder\'s setting, power and identity leadership. Day 20: conformity, engaged followership, groupthink and polarisation make dissent costly, while a consistent minority can reopen a question. Day 21: conspiracy stories satisfy needs for certainty, control and belonging, but epistemic vigilance asks for sources, disconfirmation and proportionate action. Intervene privately: keep the relationship, ask what evidence could change their mind, map hours, health and financial risk, speak with a trusted outsider, and create a reversible exit plan. No ridicule, no counter-cult certainty. The aim is agency plus evidence, not winning a debate.'),
